{"qid":"Q45670","names":["Kingdom of Portugal"],"title":"Kingdom of Portugal","endonym":{"native":"Reino de Portugal","lang":"pt"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Portugal emerged from the County of Portugal when Afonso Henriques declared independence from the Kingdom of León and won recognition as king following his victory at Ourique (1139); papal recognition followed in 1179. Over the next two centuries Portuguese kings consolidated the Reconquista along the Atlantic coast, capturing Lisbon in 1147 and completing the reconquest of the Algarve by 1249. The 15th and 16th centuries brought maritime empire—Vasco da Gama's voyage to India (1498), the Estado da India, and the Brazilian captaincies—making Portugal the first global commercial empire. The monarchy survived the Iberian Union with Spain (1580–1640), the Napoleonic invasions, and the liberal constitutional struggles of the 19th century before the republican revolution of 5 October 1910 abolished the dynasty and proclaimed a republic.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Metropolitan Portuguese boundaries are well-attested through medieval charters and 19th-century surveys; colonial extents are documented in treaty records (Tordesillas 1494, subsequent Anglo-Portuguese treaties) but overseas territories shifted frequently and are approximate for early periods.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Portugal","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Portugal/History"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Portugal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Portugal"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Portugal","rulers":[{"qid